WHERE IT COMES FROM

picowallet, by Austin Griffith (https://github.com/austintgriffith/picowallet), MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 Austin Griffith.
  PicoQuorum began as a fork of picowallet and is inspired by it: picowallet showed that a
  Raspberry Pi Pico can drive a secure element from MicroPython, rebuild an EIP-712 digest on the
  device, show it, and sign with a key that never leaves the chip. These files started there, and
  each says so on its first line:
    firmware/atecc.py, trustm.py, p256.py, keccak.py, lcd.py, blockies.py, net.py
    emu/core/runtime.mjs, png.mjs, workspace.mjs, ship.mjs, devices.mjs, usbinfo.py
    emu/core/shims/machine.py, _bootstrap.py, network.py, requests.py, socket.py, rp2.py
    emu/headless.mjs, emu/cli.mjs, emu/server.mjs, emu/web/app.js, app.css, index.html, worker.js
    tools/emu, tools/check_config
  They are under the MIT license in LICENSE, which carries Austin Griffith's copyright. Fork
  point: upstream commit 87c717c (2026-09-13); upstream merged through 72539d5 (2026-09-24). See
  docs/UPSTREAM-TRACKING.md.

ATECC608-demo (https://github.com/clawdbotatg/ATECC608-demo): the Raspberry Pi signer picowallet
  grew out of. The legacy-test config table in firmware/atecc.py is byte for byte the one its
  provision.py wrote, which is Microchip's ATECC608 reference configuration from cryptoauthlib's
  tests (test_ecc608_configdata).

MicroPython (https://micropython.org), MIT, Copyright (c) 2013-2025 Damien P. George and others:
  the WebAssembly build the emulator and the console page run, in docs/console/vendor/micropython/
  with its notice.

Atkinson Hyperlegible Next and Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono (Braille Institute of America,
  https://github.com/googlefonts/atkinson-hyperlegible-next), SIL Open Font License 1.1: the site's
  fonts in docs/app/fonts/ and docs/console/fonts/, and the console's glyphs in firmware/qf_*.bin,
  which tools/fonts/gen.py renders from them. The license is in tools/fonts/OFL.txt and beside the
  fonts.

viem, @noble/curves and @noble/hashes, MIT: bundled into docs/app/app.js, with their notices at
  the end of that file.

Blockies (https://github.com/ethereum/blockies): firmware/blockies.py and web/src/blockies.mjs
  draw the same identicons, ported from its algorithm.

The root certificates in firmware/ca_*.bin are Amazon's and Starfield's public CA certificates.

